.TH compress "" "Command"
.SH NAME
compress, uncompress, zcat \- compress and expand data
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBcompress [\-cCdfnqvV] [\-b \fImaxbits\fB] [\-w \fIworkfile\fB] [\fIfile ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
compress -- reduce file size using modified Lempel-Ziv encoding
Usage:	compress [ -cCdfnqvV ] [ -b maxbits ] [ -w workfile ] [ file ... ]
Options:
	-b maxbits	Limit the number of bits per code (default: 12)
	-c	Write output to standard output
	-C	Generate output compatible with compress 2.0
	-d	Decompress instead of compress
	-f	Force output file, even if no space would be saved
	-n	Omit the magic-number header
	-q	Quiet: suppress compression statistics (default)
	-v	Verbose: print compression statistics
	-V	Print version and build options
	-w workfile	Use workfile for scratch paging instead of a file under /tmp
.HE
.B compress
reduces the size of each
.I file
using the Lempel-Ziv (LZW) adaptive coding technique, and
.B uncompress
reverses the process.
If no
.I file
is named,
.B compress
reads the standard input and writes the compressed result to the
standard output.
.PP
When
.I file
arguments are given,
.B compress
replaces each one with a compressed file of the same name plus a
.B .Z
suffix, and copies the original file's mode, owner, group, and
access and modification times onto it; the original is then
removed.
.B uncompress
does the reverse: it expects each
.I file
to end in
.BR .Z ,
appending that suffix if it is missing, and writes the expanded
data to a file with the
.B .Z
suffix stripped.
.PP
.B compress
run under the name
.B uncompress
behaves as though
.B \-d
had been given; run under the name
.BR zcat ,
it behaves as though
.B \-d
and
.B \-c
had both been given.
.B uncompress
and
.B zcat
are the same binary as
.B compress
under different names.
.PP
A file already ending in
.B .Z
is left alone when given to
.B compress
for compression, with a message that it already has that suffix.
If compressing a file would not save space, the
.B .Z
file is removed and the original is left unchanged, unless
.B \-f
was given.
.IP \fB\-b\fImaxbits\fR
Limit the width of the variable-length codes written during
compression to
.I maxbits
bits (9 to 12 by default; wider limits use more memory and can
compress somewhat better).
This build supports code widths up to 16 bits for decompression,
but a compression request wider than 15 bits is reduced to 15.
This option affects only compression;
.B uncompress
always uses whatever code width is recorded in the file's header.
.IP \fB\-c\fR
Write the compressed or decompressed data to the standard output
instead of to a
.B .Z
file, and leave the input file untouched.
.IP \fB\-C\fR
Generate output compatible with
.B compress
version 2.0, by disabling the adaptive table-clearing (block
compression) scheme.
.IP \fB\-d\fR
Decompress instead of compress; equivalent to invoking
.B compress
as
.BR uncompress .
.IP \fB\-f\fR
Force an output file to be produced, even if one already exists
(overwriting it without asking) or if compression would not
reduce the file's size.
Without
.BR \-f ,
.B compress
asks before overwriting an existing output file if standard error
is a terminal, and otherwise leaves the existing file alone.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
When compressing, do not write the 3-byte magic-number header.
When decompressing, do not expect it.
Useful for files produced by very old versions of
.BR compress .
.IP \fB\-q\fR
Quiet: do not report a compression ratio.
This is the default.
.IP \fB\-v\fR
Verbose: report the compression ratio achieved for each file, or
for the standard input.
.IP \fB\-V\fR
Print the program's version string and the options it was built
with (for example the maximum code width), then continue
processing normally.
.IP \fB\-w\fIworkfile\fR
Use
.I workfile
to hold the compression tables that do not fit in the small
in-memory cache, instead of a temporary file created under
.BR /tmp .
This matters only when
.I maxbits
is greater than 12.
If
.I workfile
names a block or character special device, and that device does
not appear to already hold a file system,
.B compress
uses it directly instead of creating an ordinary file on it.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 16n
\fB/tmp/czXXXXXX\fR	default scratch file for -w when maxbits > 12
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B cat
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B compress
exits 0 on success; 1 if a named file is not a regular file, has
other links, or if an argument or input file is malformed (missing
option argument, bad magic number, or a code width the binary
cannot handle); and 2 if compressing a file did not save space and
.B \-f
was not given, in which case the input file is left unchanged and
no
.B .Z
file is kept.
.SH NOTES
An interrupt or an unexpected fault while decompressing removes the
partially written output file rather than leaving a truncated one
behind.
